1
00:00:03,863 --> 00:00:06,384
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is Majjhima Nikāya, 150.

2
00:00:08,065 --> 00:00:14,408
[SPEAKER_00]: To the Nāgara, Nāgara Vidans, some tribe of people.

3
00:00:14,688 --> 00:00:15,009
[SPEAKER_01]: Which one?

4
00:00:15,029 --> 00:00:15,769
[SPEAKER_01]: 150?

5
00:00:15,889 --> 00:00:16,089
[SPEAKER_01]: 50.

6
00:00:16,309 --> 00:00:16,870
[SPEAKER_00]: Uh-huh.

7
00:00:16,910 --> 00:00:17,170
[SPEAKER_00]: 150.

8
00:00:18,631 --> 00:00:27,135
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, have I heard, on one occasion, the Blessed One was wandering the Kausalan country with a large saṅgha of bhikkhus and eventually arrived to this Kausalan village named Nāgara Vida.

9
00:00:29,316 --> 00:00:31,858
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, the Brahmin householders of that village

10
00:00:32,772 --> 00:00:37,756
[SPEAKER_00]: I heard that the Buddha had come, and thought, now it is good to see such arahants.

11
00:00:38,176 --> 00:00:39,016
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I went to visit him.

12
00:00:40,838 --> 00:00:57,029
[SPEAKER_00]: And when the Brahmins, householders, went to visit the Blessed One, some paid homage to him, others exchanged greetings, sat down, some extended their hands in salutation, some were silent, and so on.

13
00:00:57,049 --> 00:01:01,433
[SPEAKER_00]: When they were seated, the Blessed One said to them, He addressed them.

14
00:01:03,858 --> 00:01:13,523
[SPEAKER_00]: Householders, if wanderers of other sects asked you thus, so other priests or other religious ascetics and so on, monks and that.

15
00:01:14,483 --> 00:01:21,887
[SPEAKER_00]: Householders, what kind of ascetics and Brahmins should not be honored, respected, revered and venerated?

16
00:01:23,887 --> 00:01:25,228
[SPEAKER_00]: You should answer them thus.

17
00:01:26,229 --> 00:01:33,472
[SPEAKER_00]: Those recluses and Brahmins who are not rid of lust, hate and delusion regarding forms cognizable

18
00:01:33,977 --> 00:01:34,497
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is that?

19
00:01:54,261 --> 00:01:59,945
[SPEAKER_00]: Because we ourselves are not rid of lust and hate and delusion regarding forms cognizable by the eye.

20
00:02:00,725 --> 00:02:07,789
[SPEAKER_00]: Our minds are not inwardly peaceful and we conduct ourselves now righteously, now unrighteously in body, speech and mind.

21
00:02:08,350 --> 00:02:17,816
[SPEAKER_00]: Since we do not see any higher righteous conduct on the part of those good recluses and Brahmins, they should not be honored and respected, revered and venerated.

22
00:02:17,856 --> 00:02:21,318
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what the Buddha says they should... they should regard things.

23
00:02:29,794 --> 00:02:50,183
[SPEAKER_00]: You should say, those recluses and brahmins who are rid of lust, hate, delusion, regarding forms, cognizable by the eye, whose minds are inwardly peaceful, and who conduct themselves righteously in body, speech, and mind.

24
00:02:53,741 --> 00:02:57,644
[SPEAKER_00]: Such recluses and Brahmins should be honored, respected, revered, and venerated.

25
00:02:57,824 --> 00:02:58,384
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is that?

26
00:02:59,145 --> 00:03:01,826
[SPEAKER_00]: Because we ourselves are not rid of lust, hate, and delusion.

27
00:03:03,868 --> 00:03:10,372
[SPEAKER_00]: Our minds are not inwardly peaceful and we conduct ourselves, now righteously, now unrighteously, in body, speech, and mind.

28
00:03:11,213 --> 00:03:17,637
[SPEAKER_00]: Since we see higher righteous conduct on the part of those good recluses and Brahmins, they should be honored, respected, and revered.

29
00:03:24,225 --> 00:03:37,295
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, he asks householders, if wanderers of other sects ask you thus, but what are your reasons and what is your evidence regarding those venerable ones whereby you say about them?

30
00:03:38,396 --> 00:03:43,579
[SPEAKER_00]: Surely, these venerable ones are either rid of lust or practicing for the removal of lust.

31
00:03:43,599 --> 00:03:47,082
[SPEAKER_00]: They are either rid of hate or are practicing for the removal of hate.

32
00:03:47,763 --> 00:03:51,986
[SPEAKER_00]: They are either rid of delusion or are practicing for the removal of delusion.

33
00:03:53,455 --> 00:03:53,955
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

34
00:03:53,996 --> 00:04:01,522
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't come and sit in the middle of the village waiting for guests and visitors and engagements and whatnot.

35
00:04:15,491 --> 00:04:16,612
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, what would your proof be?

36
00:04:16,632 --> 00:04:17,894
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you don't know those people.

37
00:04:18,455 --> 00:04:21,879
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why would you assume that they are free of these things?

38
00:04:22,099 --> 00:04:26,104
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, if not free, then they're at least practicing for it.

39
00:04:26,164 --> 00:04:26,404
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

40
00:04:26,545 --> 00:04:32,772
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the opposite of remote jungle dwellings is that which is founded in greed, aversion, delusion.

41
00:04:33,052 --> 00:04:33,353
[SPEAKER_01]: People

42
00:04:34,242 --> 00:04:35,563
[SPEAKER_01]: Inhabited areas.

43
00:04:35,603 --> 00:04:36,203
[SPEAKER_01]: Accessible.

44
00:04:36,843 --> 00:04:41,485
[SPEAKER_01]: Accessibility, obviously, to sensuality, to this... well, to greed, aversion, delusion.

45
00:04:42,066 --> 00:04:51,930
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe they're not seeking sensuality, not seeking anything based on aversion, but they're clearly seeking distraction.

46
00:04:51,990 --> 00:04:55,272
[SPEAKER_00]: Why else would you come to people, to a village?

47
00:04:55,312 --> 00:04:56,932
[SPEAKER_01]: Why would you?

48
00:04:57,152 --> 00:04:59,914
[SPEAKER_01]: As, you know, sort of take a rest in place.

49
00:05:02,420 --> 00:05:09,324
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you come, you come for a purpose of alms rounds or something, but otherwise, why would you go into the inhabited area?

50
00:05:10,225 --> 00:05:11,606
[SPEAKER_01]: There's absolutely no reason for it.

51
00:05:12,266 --> 00:05:18,730
[SPEAKER_01]: And that there is a good criteria, then, that those who want to see what those people are practicing for, can use.

52
00:05:20,584 --> 00:05:21,104
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, okay.

53
00:05:21,364 --> 00:05:29,629
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he prefers... he prefers to stay in a remote dwelling in the forest with wild beasts than to come with people.

54
00:05:30,049 --> 00:05:31,490
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because he has issues with people.

55
00:05:31,710 --> 00:05:32,610
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't hate anyone.

56
00:05:33,071 --> 00:05:35,032
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's what he finds rest for.

57
00:05:35,372 --> 00:05:36,012
[SPEAKER_00]: Go and see him.

58
00:05:36,032 --> 00:05:37,333
[SPEAKER_00]: He's normal, like a normal guy.

59
00:05:37,353 --> 00:05:38,033
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, of course.

60
00:05:38,113 --> 00:05:38,633
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, of course.

61
00:05:38,653 --> 00:05:39,454
[SPEAKER_01]: Go and see him, eh.

62
00:05:39,973 --> 00:05:41,574
[SPEAKER_01]: He's an atta or something, sure.

63
00:05:41,614 --> 00:05:41,834
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

64
00:05:42,095 --> 00:05:46,118
[SPEAKER_01]: But, no, in this case, as the criteria, as the Buddha says, no, he would prefer that.

65
00:05:46,178 --> 00:05:58,387
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only two reasons why he, why somebody would prefer remote, and I mean like remote, I don't mean like you go and stay in a monastery or a little, little, little temple or something, because that's not remote, that's still among people.

66
00:05:58,747 --> 00:06:02,610
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you get to your own room, you're still crowded by people.

67
00:06:03,191 --> 00:06:05,973
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if nobody comes to your room, you're still with people.

68
00:06:07,743 --> 00:06:15,546
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if somebody goes and stays in the wilderness, they... and that person would prefer that for two reasons, as the Buddha said.

69
00:06:15,846 --> 00:06:21,528
[SPEAKER_01]: One, they're already either free from greed, aversion, delusion, or they're practicing for it.

70
00:06:24,949 --> 00:06:35,792
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, non-solitude is directly contradictory for practice of freedom from greed, aversion, delusion.

71
00:06:35,873 --> 00:06:37,113
[SPEAKER_01]: It's incompatible with it.

72
00:06:38,588 --> 00:06:50,717
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if somebody says, oh, yes, I'm practicing from greed, aversion, delusion, and live surrounded by twenty, thirty other people, and most of the day spends in the community of people, they don't... they're not practicing for that.

73
00:06:50,857 --> 00:06:54,459
[SPEAKER_01]: No matter what they think, they're not practicing from greed, aversion, delusion.

74
00:06:54,960 --> 00:06:57,922
[SPEAKER_01]: Because for that, solitary base is necessary.

75
00:07:01,710 --> 00:07:18,548
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like when most of the day you're actually alone, literally alone, alone, and maybe once or twice a day you come together for whatever purpose, whether it's pent-up or some meeting or something, but most of the time you're alone, and not for a week or two weeks.

76
00:07:19,469 --> 00:07:24,114
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, even that's better than nothing, but no, that's your mode of living that you prefer.

77
00:07:27,191 --> 00:07:32,996
[SPEAKER_00]: Because as householders, they would say, well, how is this ascetic living?

78
00:07:33,016 --> 00:07:38,220
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, he lives in a very good house, a mansion, a palace.

79
00:07:39,361 --> 00:07:42,604
[SPEAKER_00]: He's surrounded by 10, 20, 30 people.

80
00:07:42,944 --> 00:07:43,225
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

81
00:07:43,865 --> 00:07:45,386
[SPEAKER_01]: Many of whom act like servants.

82
00:07:45,426 --> 00:07:45,607
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

83
00:07:45,867 --> 00:07:47,388
[SPEAKER_00]: He's got many duties.

84
00:07:48,069 --> 00:07:49,590
[SPEAKER_01]: Many duties, many engagements.

85
00:07:49,770 --> 00:07:55,235
[SPEAKER_00]: So, but he does live in harmony with all those people, just like I.

86
00:07:56,758 --> 00:08:00,639
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this ascetic is living the life that I want to live as a householder.

87
00:08:00,659 --> 00:08:01,039
[SPEAKER_00]: Therefore...

88
00:08:01,079 --> 00:08:02,159
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the thing.

89
00:08:02,199 --> 00:08:09,540
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, then, you sort of... you realize that they're the only... At most, it's a slight upgrade to a household life.

90
00:08:25,868 --> 00:08:30,990
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like a commune life, spiritual commune, or whatever you want to call it.

91
00:08:31,131 --> 00:08:37,614
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a slight upgrade because... well, it would be a slight upgrade if, say, they would keep the rules, keep the eight precepts.

92
00:08:38,594 --> 00:08:42,116
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's a slight upgrade to a household life because of the eight precepts.

93
00:08:42,576 --> 00:08:44,617
[SPEAKER_01]: But, it's not a necessity.

94
00:08:46,108 --> 00:08:50,292
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, a householder can start keeping their precepts, and you already met that.

95
00:08:50,392 --> 00:08:56,937
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what it means that if you if you kind of review and see like, well, actually, no, I sometimes do that, sometimes I don't.

96
00:08:57,178 --> 00:08:57,818
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes I do.

97
00:08:57,898 --> 00:08:58,859
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, righteously, no.

98
00:08:58,999 --> 00:08:59,360
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

99
00:08:59,400 --> 00:09:05,725
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now, I can, I can, if I were only to do the precepts, then there wouldn't be a difference between me and this this commune that I venerate.

100
00:09:06,265 --> 00:09:07,086
[SPEAKER_01]: It just wouldn't.

101
00:09:07,106 --> 00:09:07,246
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

102
00:09:07,507 --> 00:09:09,388
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there is no higher distinction.

103
00:09:10,129 --> 00:09:13,632
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything else... And then, in many other regards, it's actually easier in my life

104
00:09:15,413 --> 00:09:20,958
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what if I were to then keep the precepts and I would have a relatively easier life?

105
00:09:20,998 --> 00:09:22,740
[SPEAKER_01]: But, of course, many people would not trade that.

106
00:09:22,760 --> 00:09:28,886
[SPEAKER_01]: Many people are afraid to be celibate and so on, but that's just the state of the human mind at the present age.

107
00:09:29,487 --> 00:09:35,012
[SPEAKER_01]: But the point is, you need to recognize, okay, so it's a slight improvement just because I'm so bad.

108
00:09:35,272 --> 00:09:37,695
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, I can't even keep the five precepts or eight precepts.

109
00:09:39,216 --> 00:09:39,797
[SPEAKER_00]: Sometimes I do.

110
00:09:40,527 --> 00:09:42,188
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes they do, which means, okay.

111
00:09:42,489 --> 00:09:46,312
[SPEAKER_01]: So, and yeah, sure enough, these people in the commune, sometimes they do, sometimes they don't.

112
00:09:46,612 --> 00:09:48,113
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes they leave, sometimes they come back.

113
00:09:48,894 --> 00:09:54,178
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what's the, like, non-worldly distinction, except the circumstances?

114
00:09:54,818 --> 00:09:57,160
[SPEAKER_01]: And there would be freedom from greed, aversion, delusion.

115
00:09:57,401 --> 00:10:00,143
[SPEAKER_01]: There would be practicing nibbāna and practicing towards nibbāna.

116
00:10:01,284 --> 00:10:04,186
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, so how would you set that criteria?

117
00:10:04,866 --> 00:10:06,588
[SPEAKER_01]: How do those people in commune live?

118
00:10:07,712 --> 00:10:12,353
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's a commune, means... it's... it's... it's a community, means they don't live... there's no seclusion.

119
00:10:13,274 --> 00:10:16,134
[SPEAKER_01]: Or seclusion is relative to the... to the coming together.

120
00:10:16,915 --> 00:10:18,295
[SPEAKER_01]: Mostly, it's about coming together.

121
00:10:18,875 --> 00:10:20,336
[SPEAKER_01]: Occasionally, you get time off.

122
00:10:21,236 --> 00:10:22,936
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, that's like a household life.

123
00:10:23,916 --> 00:10:30,138
[SPEAKER_01]: Occasionally, you know, I earn well, now I can go and afford a 10-day holiday on my own, or a retreat, or whatever.

124
00:10:30,758 --> 00:10:36,360
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if a householder to review his situation, he says, well, this is not a massive improvement.

125
00:10:37,142 --> 00:10:42,206
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not going to result in freedom from greed, aversion, delusion, because there is no... there's no solitude.

126
00:10:42,226 --> 00:10:43,166
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no physical solitude.

127
00:10:43,186 --> 00:10:44,207
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no remoteness.

128
00:10:44,828 --> 00:10:49,731
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no... literally, that... that being alone, most of the time.

129
00:10:50,572 --> 00:10:55,655
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's not incidental, because being alone is the hardest thing to do for a Patrujana.

130
00:10:56,736 --> 00:11:02,620
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha said, you have to develop wisdom in order to start finding pleasure in solitude.

131
00:11:03,441 --> 00:11:04,842
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, it's going to be painful.

132
00:11:05,402 --> 00:11:06,423
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the whole point.

133
00:11:07,626 --> 00:11:16,815
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the neutral feeling, if it's unknown, if it's not understood, it's unpleasant.

134
00:11:16,895 --> 00:11:18,556
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the definition of boredom.

135
00:11:19,377 --> 00:11:29,087
[SPEAKER_01]: It starts neutral, but you don't understand it, you don't understand the feeling, you want something, whatever, your craving is there, means that neutral feeling is going to be very unpleasant.

136
00:11:30,295 --> 00:11:31,836
[SPEAKER_01]: Very unpleasant, if you stay with it.

137
00:11:32,717 --> 00:11:33,278
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why...

138
00:11:33,698 --> 00:11:46,509
[SPEAKER_01]: So, and... See, that's the problem that I would often kind of go back to, when I describe these things, like with these group retreats, organized retreats, where people are perfectly silent, nothing, no engagement, no distraction, but you're still with other people.

139
00:11:46,629 --> 00:11:53,875
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, your meditation technique becomes something you do, something you perform, so you're not alone.

140
00:11:55,048 --> 00:12:05,051
[SPEAKER_01]: Being alone means having nothing to do, or very little to do throughout a day, except sit with yourself, and with your own thoughts, and with your own feelings.

141
00:12:06,271 --> 00:12:10,792
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not incidental that all the puthujjanas' effort revolve around avoiding this.

142
00:12:11,332 --> 00:12:14,113
[SPEAKER_01]: Even the puthujjanas who committed to practicing Buddhist teaching.

143
00:12:15,439 --> 00:12:29,408
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, live in massive communes, constantly frequenting people, constantly spending time with people, coming together in the morning, coming together in the afternoon, coming together in the evening, duties, duties, loads of duties, loads of stuff to do, or go for retreats where you...

144
00:12:29,429 --> 00:12:38,795
[SPEAKER_01]: I must do step one, step two, step three, watching, watching, moving, breathing, breathing, I must do this, I must do this, because if I stop doing anything, I will start sinking.

145
00:12:40,172 --> 00:12:41,954
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why people are terrified of solitude.

146
00:12:42,595 --> 00:12:49,164
[SPEAKER_01]: Even those who go and seek solitude, they then have to find something they must do in it, so they don't sink in that solitude.

147
00:12:51,848 --> 00:12:57,756
[SPEAKER_01]: And for as long as you rely on such efforts, you are not the one that's delighting in finding joy in solitude.

148
00:13:00,705 --> 00:13:02,086
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why you have to stop doing that.

149
00:13:03,046 --> 00:13:08,029
[SPEAKER_01]: Take the hit of anxiety or whatever else, even at the risk of going mad.

150
00:13:08,469 --> 00:13:14,793
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just, as the Buddha said, sometimes, samanas and brāhmaṇas, they set off to swim correctly to the other shore, but then they drown.

151
00:13:15,859 --> 00:13:17,740
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to prepare yourself with your virtue.

152
00:13:17,760 --> 00:13:23,303
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why Buddha said, don't go to forest unless your virtue has been properly developed, the teaching has been understood and so on.

153
00:13:23,844 --> 00:13:29,507
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, that doesn't mean like, okay, so I feel justified in not seeking seclusion, because I still haven't developed it.

154
00:13:29,527 --> 00:13:30,487
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, what are you waiting for?

155
00:13:32,088 --> 00:13:33,149
[SPEAKER_01]: It's on you either way.

156
00:13:36,171 --> 00:13:40,613
[SPEAKER_01]: And everything is summed up in that little passage that probably many people would have just read.

157
00:13:40,673 --> 00:13:41,314
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, of course.

158
00:13:41,354 --> 00:13:41,894
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

159
00:13:42,514 --> 00:13:43,115
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like, no.

160
00:13:43,875 --> 00:13:45,276
[SPEAKER_01]: Review again what the Buddha says there.

161
00:13:46,302 --> 00:13:50,504
[SPEAKER_01]: That kind of dwelling means freedom from greed, aversion, delusion or practice towards it.

162
00:13:51,665 --> 00:13:56,067
[SPEAKER_01]: Absence of that kind of dwelling means not practicing for freedom from greed, aversion, delusion.

163
00:13:57,068 --> 00:14:00,510
[SPEAKER_00]: No matter what you put on your poster and the advertisement of your place and what not.

164
00:14:02,591 --> 00:14:11,556
[SPEAKER_01]: So, and another, again, important thing is, so even people who do go and seek seclusion, then they have to resist that inner kind of

165
00:14:13,010 --> 00:14:14,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Thoughts cognizable by the mind.

166
00:14:14,171 --> 00:14:15,212
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

167
00:14:15,252 --> 00:14:18,674
[SPEAKER_01]: Shut my thinking or focus, focus on this, focus on this, focus on this, get experience.

168
00:14:35,782 --> 00:14:35,902
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

169
00:14:36,062 --> 00:14:43,549
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to be you in that solitude and start feeling that solitude basically swallowing you, threatening you.

170
00:14:45,311 --> 00:14:47,033
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why virtue is not negotiable.

171
00:14:47,093 --> 00:14:49,695
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to develop virtue beforehand, because then you can take it.

172
00:14:51,297 --> 00:14:54,960
[SPEAKER_01]: You can take that threat, because it's only a threat.

173
00:14:55,821 --> 00:14:58,584
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing can happen to you unless you choose to do it.

174
00:15:00,282 --> 00:15:04,626
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like a definition how basically our people would do things, when break down.

175
00:15:04,787 --> 00:15:06,628
[SPEAKER_00]: You see the danger, you see the safety.

176
00:15:06,648 --> 00:15:07,609
[SPEAKER_01]: Pressure, pressure, pressure.

177
00:15:07,690 --> 00:15:14,036
[SPEAKER_01]: But it can't... nothing can be done to you, unless things you choose to do with yourself.

178
00:15:15,087 --> 00:15:17,348
[SPEAKER_01]: On account of that pressure, on account of that fear.

179
00:15:17,768 --> 00:15:26,011
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's that sutta, the fear and dread, where the Buddha would go in this remote, dangerous dwelling with like tigers and whatnot, doing walking meditation at night.

180
00:15:26,651 --> 00:15:36,715
[SPEAKER_01]: And he said, well, if while I were walking, back and forth, pondering on the dhamma, a fear would arise because he heard something in the bushes.

181
00:15:37,235 --> 00:15:40,276
[SPEAKER_01]: He wouldn't change the posture until the fear is dispelled.

182
00:15:40,396 --> 00:15:41,497
[SPEAKER_01]: That's exactly what I'm talking about.

183
00:15:42,073 --> 00:15:45,356
[SPEAKER_01]: He wouldn't do anything on account of there isn't fear.

184
00:15:47,358 --> 00:15:53,183
[SPEAKER_01]: Until the fear is dispelled, then whatever he does won't be rooted in trying to avoid the fear.

185
00:15:54,424 --> 00:15:55,085
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

186
00:15:56,025 --> 00:15:58,427
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't go mad unless you give in to.

187
00:15:58,648 --> 00:16:01,530
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't do anything unwholesome unless you give in to.

188
00:16:03,472 --> 00:16:06,234
[SPEAKER_01]: Depression can't do anything to you unless you let it.

189
00:16:07,215 --> 00:16:09,157
[SPEAKER_00]: Unless you choose to allow it.

190
00:16:11,737 --> 00:16:19,543
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the Buddha wouldn't do until this disappears so that it doesn't become, ah, this was letting in the pressure, letting in the fear.

191
00:16:20,163 --> 00:16:38,096
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, if he were seated or standing while the fear arose, he would not change the posture, means his basis of his situation would not change, he would not do anything significant until the fear is dispelled or subsides, and then he would do it, just to make sure that it's not rooted in trying to avoid it.

192
00:16:43,692 --> 00:16:49,876
[SPEAKER_01]: But all these things will become apparent for the one who goes and starts, well, seeking solitude.

193
00:16:50,216 --> 00:16:54,619
[SPEAKER_01]: If they're not ready for it, then every single effort should be towards preparation for it.

194
00:16:57,401 --> 00:17:00,744
[SPEAKER_01]: Virtue, restraint, knowledge of the teachings.

195
00:17:01,924 --> 00:17:04,766
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, okay, so I'm in solitude.

196
00:17:04,886 --> 00:17:06,307
[SPEAKER_01]: What must I do?

197
00:17:06,327 --> 00:17:07,168
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what you should do.

198
00:17:07,528 --> 00:17:07,808
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing.

199
00:17:08,429 --> 00:17:09,710
[SPEAKER_01]: Abide in non-activity.

200
00:17:10,640 --> 00:17:15,003
[SPEAKER_01]: And start emulating it, and start seeing yourself as completely circumstantial and so on.

201
00:17:16,443 --> 00:17:17,324
[SPEAKER_01]: And you'll get used to it.

202
00:17:18,144 --> 00:17:26,849
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't break down, if you don't break the precepts, if you don't run away in fear, if you don't suddenly jump on a view and say, no, no, no, no, this is a mistake, you should go and be with people, people are good.

203
00:17:27,530 --> 00:17:31,072
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, if you don't give in, in any shape or form, you will get used to it.

204
00:17:31,652 --> 00:17:32,753
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you'll start to see it.

205
00:17:33,333 --> 00:17:35,054
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what the Buddha said in the Radha-sutta.

206
00:17:38,893 --> 00:17:40,575
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the other sutra.

207
00:17:40,595 --> 00:17:55,831
[SPEAKER_01]: It says, a monk who is basically living in the company, living in the commune, so to speak, living with others, and he obviously values it.

208
00:17:57,613 --> 00:18:10,144
[SPEAKER_01]: finds finds it peaceful, finds it delightful, finds it contentful to live like that, that he will develop pleasure of seclusion, that's not possible.

209
00:18:11,184 --> 00:18:22,554
[SPEAKER_01]: And for the monk who lives in the company, who because of that cannot develop pleasure of seclusion, that he will then develop sign of his mind, discern the sign of the mind, that's impossible.

210
00:18:23,457 --> 00:18:30,362
[SPEAKER_01]: The one who cannot discern the sign of the mind, because he doesn't live in seclusion, because he delights in company, that he will then develop the right view.

211
00:18:30,883 --> 00:18:31,764
[SPEAKER_01]: That's impossible.

212
00:18:33,385 --> 00:18:36,427
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, without the right view, to know basically what dhamma is, that's impossible.

213
00:18:36,447 --> 00:18:38,949
[SPEAKER_01]: Without knowing what dhamma is, to develop nibbāna, that's also impossible.

214
00:18:39,429 --> 00:18:41,871
[SPEAKER_01]: All because he delights in company.

215
00:18:41,931 --> 00:18:46,875
[SPEAKER_01]: All because he's never dealt with his personal solitude for himself.

216
00:18:47,696 --> 00:18:50,078
[SPEAKER_01]: And I don't mean he pushed himself for ten days, you know.

217
00:18:51,712 --> 00:18:54,293
[SPEAKER_01]: Not talking to and not seeing anybody, just some... No.

218
00:18:54,633 --> 00:18:57,634
[SPEAKER_01]: Actually, found the joy in it and start preferring it.

219
00:18:57,714 --> 00:19:04,957
[SPEAKER_01]: Not done it as a novelty, kind of little, little, to wind himself up or something, like some, some intense retreat.

220
00:19:05,157 --> 00:19:05,317
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

221
00:19:05,557 --> 00:19:09,319
[SPEAKER_01]: Actually, found the joy in it and then starts to prefer it.

222
00:19:13,551 --> 00:19:22,099
[SPEAKER_01]: And in order for a person to do that, they need to go through that, that layer of anxiety and free of being alone without having anything to do and so on.

223
00:19:23,580 --> 00:19:25,362
[SPEAKER_01]: But that is, that is the gateway.

224
00:19:25,442 --> 00:19:30,987
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if somebody does that, then they'll start finding the pleasure in solitude.

225
00:19:31,488 --> 00:19:39,195
[SPEAKER_01]: If somebody starts finding pleasure in solitude, they'll start to discern the peripheral signs of their own mind as a phenomenon, not their own doing.

226
00:19:40,129 --> 00:19:52,551
[SPEAKER_01]: If one starts discerning the peripheral signs of their own mind, he can then develop the right view and become a Sotāpanna, because he knows that that's where the practice is, that's where the suttas apply, that's where all the things the Buddha talks about should be discerned.

227
00:19:52,651 --> 00:19:56,152
[SPEAKER_01]: He sees the area where... he sees the domain, basically, of it.

228
00:19:58,632 --> 00:20:07,714
[SPEAKER_01]: But without going through that personal terror of anxiety, of boredom, to arrive at it, that's not possible.

229
00:20:08,608 --> 00:20:13,452
[SPEAKER_01]: And all of that is rooted in company, or rooted in seclusion.

230
00:20:14,532 --> 00:20:18,095
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, such a categorical criteria.

231
00:20:19,176 --> 00:20:23,959
[SPEAKER_01]: If they seek such dwellings, they're either like that, or practicing for the freedom of that.

232
00:20:24,620 --> 00:20:32,685
[SPEAKER_01]: If they don't, they're not like that, and not practicing for the freedom from that.

233
00:20:32,966 --> 00:20:36,068
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even the sotāpannas, who were like lay people back in the day,

234
00:20:36,992 --> 00:20:49,058
[SPEAKER_01]: They dealt with that personal existential pressure of solitude, boredom, without resorting to a meditation technique, without resorting to a wrong view, without resorting to company, without resorting to distraction.

235
00:20:49,198 --> 00:20:51,419
[SPEAKER_01]: They dealt with it, they've seen it, they understood it.

236
00:20:52,779 --> 00:21:01,083
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, they chose maybe to not practice and went back because they had duties for their lay life, but that's a completely different topic that can be discussed elsewhere.

237
00:21:01,363 --> 00:21:04,365
[SPEAKER_01]: But even they, they're not like he was in the middle of his sort of

238
00:21:05,963 --> 00:21:07,103
[SPEAKER_01]: That's also why it's, eh...

239
00:21:07,183 --> 00:21:09,844
[SPEAKER_01]: I read a question that, eh... it has a Sotāpanna fear of death.

240
00:21:25,437 --> 00:21:29,859
[SPEAKER_00]: And I'll say, no, because his right view involves understanding death.

241
00:21:30,079 --> 00:21:30,519
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, exactly.

242
00:21:30,799 --> 00:21:32,320
[SPEAKER_00]: But he fears the dying.

243
00:21:32,360 --> 00:21:37,062
[SPEAKER_01]: That's... when I talk about that existential anxiety of boredom and being alone, that's... that's where death is.

244
00:21:38,162 --> 00:21:44,085
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why people are terrified of death, because... because... that's why people are terrified of... of... of that... of boredom as well.

245
00:21:44,345 --> 00:21:45,505
[SPEAKER_01]: They start losing their mind.

246
00:21:46,986 --> 00:21:54,209
[SPEAKER_01]: Normal people, perfectly functional people, you lock them alone, without any, or put them in some remote dwelling with, like, on their own.

247
00:21:57,158 --> 00:21:57,798
[SPEAKER_01]: That's exactly why.

248
00:21:57,818 --> 00:21:59,459
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's where the beginning is.

249
00:21:59,519 --> 00:22:06,560
[SPEAKER_01]: Understanding that nature of that, which is understanding the nature of death, understanding the nature of neutral feeling, the delighting in solitude.

250
00:22:06,761 --> 00:22:07,541
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all the same thing.

251
00:22:08,961 --> 00:22:13,662
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, nibbāna... That's why he often said, if he doesn't become an Arjuna, he might become in the moment of death.

252
00:22:14,062 --> 00:22:16,463
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the direction of death is the direction of the right view.

253
00:22:16,803 --> 00:22:19,704
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the direction of dispassion, disenchantment, letting go.

254
00:22:19,744 --> 00:22:20,804
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the direction of nibbāna.

255
00:22:22,164 --> 00:22:23,785
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why puthujjana fears nibbāna.

256
00:22:25,278 --> 00:22:32,584
[SPEAKER_01]: From Bhutujana's point of view, nibbāna is closer to death than to what Bhutujana thinks nibbāna is.

257
00:22:34,646 --> 00:22:40,772
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the true peace, true absence of suffering, is when you have relinquished your life, while you're still alive.

258
00:22:45,096 --> 00:22:46,677
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's the dying part, so...

259
00:22:47,948 --> 00:22:49,229
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

260
00:22:49,389 --> 00:22:51,830
[SPEAKER_01]: Death for somebody who hasn't understood death is death.

261
00:22:52,430 --> 00:22:55,632
[SPEAKER_01]: But you understood death means you're not subject to death.

262
00:22:55,712 --> 00:22:59,394
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why for an arahant, the word death is never used in the suttas.

263
00:22:59,414 --> 00:23:01,395
[SPEAKER_01]: It was used like his aggregates would break apart.

264
00:23:02,842 --> 00:23:15,399
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the many, many of people who come and hear the Buddha would, like, get, get, like, shock, terror, and anxiety, and, you know, figuratively start pulling their hair, or maybe literally, fall on the ground, beat their breasts, or whatever, kill us.

265
00:23:15,879 --> 00:23:17,501
[SPEAKER_01]: Your teaching will destroy me.

266
00:23:18,002 --> 00:23:24,225
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yeah, from somebody who is not relinquishing the self-view, what the Buddha teaches is the destruction of the self-view.

267
00:23:24,865 --> 00:23:29,687
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, through extinguishment, not through the actual destruction of it, because it was a mistake to begin with.

268
00:23:30,067 --> 00:23:31,147
[SPEAKER_01]: But Kutujana doesn't know that.

269
00:23:31,167 --> 00:23:33,908
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for him, for him, nibbāna is death, basically.

270
00:23:33,928 --> 00:23:35,209
[SPEAKER_01]: When he sees it, he fears it.

271
00:23:36,308 --> 00:23:49,935
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why that solitude, as I said, you need to get, gain wisdom, first restraint, sense restraint, virtue, and then wisdom, to start finding solitude pleasurable, correctly.

272
00:23:49,975 --> 00:23:51,395
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it comes out of that wisdom.

273
00:23:52,256 --> 00:23:56,838
[SPEAKER_01]: It's completely opposite of your entire experience of Abhidhūjana, going with the senses, going with the grain.

274
00:24:04,467 --> 00:24:11,212
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's go back to that, why they should think that someone's worthy of respect and honor.

275
00:24:12,112 --> 00:24:20,538
[SPEAKER_00]: It is because those venerable ones resort to remote jungle resting places in the forest, uninhabited forests.

276
00:24:21,939 --> 00:24:28,964
[SPEAKER_00]: For there are no forms cognizable by the eye there of a kind that they could look at and delight in.

277
00:24:30,445 --> 00:24:31,366
[SPEAKER_00]: There are no sounds.

278
00:24:31,993 --> 00:24:45,937
[SPEAKER_00]: Cognizable by the ear, they're of a kind that they could listen to and delight in, or smells, or tastes, or touches, of a kind that they could touch and delight in.

279
00:24:45,997 --> 00:24:46,957
[SPEAKER_00]: These are our reasons.

280
00:24:48,158 --> 00:24:57,240
[SPEAKER_00]: This is our evidence whereby we say about those venerable ones, surely these venerable ones are either rid of lust, hate and delusion, or practicing for the removal.

281
00:25:00,121 --> 00:25:00,361
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

282
00:25:01,541 --> 00:25:06,382
[SPEAKER_01]: See how much is contained in that little sutta, in that little few lines.

283
00:25:06,982 --> 00:25:10,263
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, okay, of course, yeah, everybody, you just read over it, of course, yeah, yeah, yeah.

284
00:25:11,283 --> 00:25:25,486
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, the entire, the entire existential dread and suffering, need for company, need for something to do, dependence on something to keep you flowing, basically, floating, so you don't sink, in yourself, it's all in there.

285
00:25:26,826 --> 00:25:28,927
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly what that criteria is rooted in.

286
00:25:29,962 --> 00:25:33,585
[SPEAKER_01]: If the person is in the environment that supports that, he's not practicing for freedom from it.

287
00:25:34,125 --> 00:25:43,773
[SPEAKER_00]: If the person is in the environment that doesn't support any distraction, any sensuality, any things that you could delight in, then he's practicing for it.

288
00:25:43,813 --> 00:25:46,435
[SPEAKER_00]: Because otherwise, he wouldn't be spending time in that area.

289
00:25:46,475 --> 00:25:47,115
[SPEAKER_00]: He would go mad.

290
00:25:47,675 --> 00:25:48,176
[SPEAKER_00]: He would leave.

291
00:25:48,296 --> 00:25:48,676
[SPEAKER_00]: He wouldn't.

292
00:25:48,956 --> 00:25:50,598
[SPEAKER_01]: But yet, he's there, full of joy.

293
00:25:58,609 --> 00:26:02,071
[SPEAKER_01]: So, a person needs to learn how to be alone with oneself.

294
00:26:04,092 --> 00:26:11,656
[SPEAKER_01]: Overcome the anxiety that comes from it, the pressure of boredom, the fear, not do anything to compromise the virtue.

295
00:26:12,977 --> 00:26:16,599
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, the signs of the mind will begin to manifest.

296
00:26:18,279 --> 00:26:19,420
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you can develop an idea.

297
00:26:19,940 --> 00:26:20,381
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing else.

298
00:26:21,141 --> 00:26:22,522
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no other gateway, by the way.

299
00:26:23,202 --> 00:26:24,423
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like there's just one way.

300
00:26:25,407 --> 00:26:30,848
[SPEAKER_01]: When the Buddha said, the reason for the right view is the reason, is the presence of the side of the mind, discerned side of the mind.

301
00:26:31,308 --> 00:26:37,469
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the side of the mind can be discerned without having went through that existential solitude.

302
00:26:37,809 --> 00:26:38,529
[SPEAKER_01]: That's impossible.

303
00:26:39,150 --> 00:26:44,411
[SPEAKER_00]: Someone might say, well, that's the introvert's way, but there's the extrovert's way.

304
00:26:44,431 --> 00:26:45,691
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is the pathogenous way.

305
00:26:46,411 --> 00:26:50,632
[SPEAKER_00]: The path of community, production of things, duties.

306
00:26:50,912 --> 00:26:51,952
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, the Buddha told of

307
00:26:54,519 --> 00:27:01,244
[SPEAKER_01]: A noble saṅgha, basically, Ananda was there, probably arahants, you know, he wasn't just with random villagers.

308
00:27:01,484 --> 00:27:15,153
[SPEAKER_01]: It was a group of monks, probably many of them, already enlightened, or at least sotāpanas, and they were sewing robes for the kathina, and discussing and doing things, preparing, and the Buddha walked in and said, what is this?

309
00:27:15,294 --> 00:27:16,154
[SPEAKER_00]: What is this noise?

310
00:27:16,234 --> 00:27:16,695
[SPEAKER_00]: What is this?

311
00:27:17,415 --> 00:27:18,596
[SPEAKER_01]: What is this crowd here?

312
00:27:19,848 --> 00:27:23,871
[SPEAKER_01]: And he says, oh, well, Lord, we are sewing our robes for the kathina, you know, which is an auspicious occasion.

313
00:27:24,511 --> 00:27:29,595
[SPEAKER_01]: And he said, well, I thought he was like a band of fishermen selling their product on the market.

314
00:27:30,495 --> 00:27:38,961
[SPEAKER_01]: And then he told them off for just dwelling in company, even for seemingly good reasons, to arahants.

315
00:27:40,082 --> 00:27:47,527
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if arahants and and and noble noble saṅgha was reprimanded for dwelling in company, what is to be said of two jhānas?

316
00:27:49,157 --> 00:28:01,924
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they could still theoretically, that company, once you get the right view, you could still theoretically, obviously much slower, you will progress much slower, but you still could progress because you got the criteria, you went through that existential solitude.

317
00:28:03,665 --> 00:28:10,648
[SPEAKER_01]: But let alone those who never even start, who from the start say, no, that's a wrong direction to go, you must stay together.

318
00:28:11,008 --> 00:28:12,829
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly why they stay together.

319
00:28:12,849 --> 00:28:17,592
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the whole emphasis on the commune and the group and group this and group this, so people will not sink.

320
00:28:18,802 --> 00:28:19,442
[SPEAKER_01]: In themselves.

321
00:28:21,003 --> 00:28:24,343
[SPEAKER_01]: But, without the risk of sinking, how can you learn to swim?

322
00:28:25,444 --> 00:28:28,124
[SPEAKER_01]: Without learning to swim, how can you cross the shore?

323
00:28:29,565 --> 00:28:30,705
[SPEAKER_00]: That's simply not possible.

324
00:28:31,525 --> 00:28:33,386
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, the risk of sinking is real.

325
00:28:34,906 --> 00:28:40,988
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, the rewards of crossing the shore are equally real, as in insurmountable.

326
00:28:42,088 --> 00:28:42,308
[SPEAKER_01]: So,

327
00:28:43,561 --> 00:28:46,642
[SPEAKER_01]: Is the fear of sinking going to prevent you from ever trying to practice correctly?

328
00:28:46,662 --> 00:28:47,902
[SPEAKER_00]: You're already sinking, kind of.

329
00:28:48,122 --> 00:28:48,942
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, exactly.

330
00:28:49,082 --> 00:28:50,422
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I'm getting at.

331
00:28:50,502 --> 00:28:50,883
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

332
00:28:51,503 --> 00:28:56,484
[SPEAKER_01]: You ask yourself, well, okay, I might not, like, try to swim because I might sink, but what's the alternative?

333
00:28:56,584 --> 00:28:57,744
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll stay on this shore.

334
00:28:57,924 --> 00:28:58,624
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll drown anyway.

335
00:28:58,664 --> 00:28:58,864
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

336
00:29:00,705 --> 00:29:03,845
[SPEAKER_01]: So, really, you don't really have much to lose.

337
00:29:05,806 --> 00:29:07,466
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, you don't want to be foolish about it.

338
00:29:07,546 --> 00:29:10,167
[SPEAKER_01]: As I said, virtue needs to be developed beforehand.

339
00:29:10,207 --> 00:29:11,247
[SPEAKER_01]: As the Buddha said himself,

340
00:29:12,135 --> 00:29:16,538
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you go to the forest dwellings and the mall and be physically secluded.

341
00:29:22,282 --> 00:29:24,364
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't just try and jump to... No.

342
00:29:24,464 --> 00:29:24,784
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

343
00:29:24,844 --> 00:29:25,044
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

344
00:29:25,084 --> 00:29:28,907
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, suddenly, shut everything down, lock in, just run into the mountains.

345
00:29:28,927 --> 00:29:29,908
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you're not going to survive.

346
00:29:32,309 --> 00:29:37,073
[SPEAKER_01]: But, not seeing that that is your task, well, that's probably the worst view you can have.

347
00:29:37,833 --> 00:29:38,394
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, no, no.

348
00:29:38,534 --> 00:29:39,094
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't need to.

349
00:29:39,154 --> 00:29:39,995
[SPEAKER_01]: No, this is fine.

350
00:29:41,247 --> 00:29:42,991
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, stay surrounded with 50 people.

351
00:29:45,216 --> 00:29:48,464
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether it's a householder or a slightly upgraded householder in a community.

